# Host (pacman) packages for shiori only, layered on ./packages by ./deploy.
# Same format and the same enforcement: one package per line, # comments, and
# every deploy converges pacman onto it.
#
# The shared list is "must be present for this config to work" on all four Arch
# hosts. This one is for things a single box wants, which is what keeps steam
# and a gigabyte of 32-bit graphics stack off headless fujiwara and off boxes
# that never asked for it. A new per-host file needs nothing but its name:
# ./packages.<hostname>, picked up by pacman_wants in ./pacman.sh.
#
# Not pacstrapped. install-base.sh reads ./packages and nothing else, and that
# is deliberate rather than an oversight: multilib is not enabled in the live
# ISO's config for the target root, so a pacstrap of steam would fail the whole
# install. A rebuilt shiori simply picks this up on its first `system/deploy`.
#
# Everything here still owes the same justification as the shared list -- a user
# profile cannot provide it. For steam that is the 32-bit stack: it is a 32-bit
# bootstrap that dlopens the host's own libGL/Vulkan ICDs, so it needs multilib
# drivers built against the host's mesa. nixpkgs' steam is an FHS env that
# resolves those against NixOS paths, which on Arch means nixGL, whose 32-bit
# support is the part that does not hold up.

# Steam, and the 32-bit halves of the drivers already installed 64-bit in
# ./packages (mesa) and by hand (vulkan-intel). Listed explicitly rather than
# left to steam's dependencies: this file is the desired state, and a driver
# that arrives only as somebody else's dep silently leaves when they do.
steam
lib32-mesa
# Panther Lake / Arc B390 is mesa's ANV, same as the 64-bit vulkan-intel.
lib32-vulkan-intel
lib32-vulkan-icd-loader

# Thermal daemon, and the reason it is here rather than in the shared list: it
# is Intel-only. It drives the ACPI DPTF devices (int3400/int3403) and the
# processor_thermal_device RAPL knobs, and on a non-Intel CPU it exits -- so on
# utsuho and fujiwara it would be a unit that fails on every boot, which is the
# one way a wrongly-placed package is not merely inert.
#
# It is the recommendation for this box, not a preference: ArchWiki's Framework
# Laptop 13 page names thermald for the Intel version under "Lowering fan
# noise", both distributions Framework certifies the Laptop 13 Pro against
# (Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Fedora 44) ship it by default on Intel, and Phoronix ran
# its Panther Lake performance-profile numbers with it applied. It sits under
# power-profiles-daemon rather than replacing it -- ppd owns the EPP and the
# ACPI platform_profile, thermald the passive trip points and the fan response.
#
# Installing it does not start it, and nothing in a package list can: ./deploy
# runs ./thermald-setup right after this step for that half.
#
# setsuna is the other Intel box. It has no ./packages.setsuna yet, and giving
# it one is the whole edit if it ever wants this.
thermald

# WireGuard client tooling for the UCG's "Kon WireGuard" overlay: this host
# reaches utsuho (10.9.0.6, the waypipe pair in home.nix) over WG because
# utsuho's tailscaled is on the work tailnet and the two tailnets never meet.
# Only the tooling lives here -- the keypair and wg0.conf stay on the host at
# /etc/wireguard (0600), not in this repo or sops, the same call the kube repo
# makes for its nodes' WG keys; peer registration is by hand in the UniFi UI.
# ./deploy enables wg-quick@wg0 once that config exists.
wireguard-tools
